Taking 14,050 households as an example, the basic load (Basic Load) is first considered.

In heating load calculations, the first household is calculated at 100% of the maximum user capacity, while the remaining users are calculated at corresponding proportions.

The remaining portion is calculated based on the maximum load. For example:

A single household is calculated at 100%;

The next two households at 65%;

The following two households at 40%;

The subsequent 15 households all at 25%;

Beyond 20 households, a uniform rate of 10% applies.

Thus, the final coefficient obtained is relatively large. This constitutes the total base load.

Additionally, the following must be calculated:
- Heating load;
- Electric vehicle charging load;
- Load for public areas.

Total load is the sum of the base load, heating load, electric vehicle charging load, and public area load.
